<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfonsine Tables</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1252–<a href="/years/1270">1270</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alfonsine Tables were astronomical tables for calculating the positions of the Sun, Moon and planets, compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso X of Castile</a> by the Jewish scholars Isaac ibn Sid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> ben <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>. Spread in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> versions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and first printed in 1483, they remained the standard tables in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> until the Copernican Prutenic Tables of 1551.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonsine-tables.jpg" alt="Alfonsine Tables" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tablas_alfonsies.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Alfonsine Tables, in Spanish Tablas Alfonsíes and in Latin Tabulae Alphonsinae, gave the data needed to compute the positions of the Sun, Moon and planets relative to the fixed stars. They are named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso X of Castile</a>, who sponsored them, and were compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>; their data begin on 1 June 1252, the date of the king's coronation. They were made as an addition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledan-tables">Toledan Tables</a>, an eleventh-century work, to improve the computation of mean motion. Alfonso gathered a team of scholars, among them Jewish astronomers such as Yehuda ben Moshe and Isaac ibn Sid, who also drew on earlier Islamic astronomical works and on observations made in Toledo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The tables kept the framework and the Ptolemaic methods of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledan-tables">Toledan Tables</a>: the geocentric model, mathematical prediction of planetary motion and calculation from tables. The authors concentrated on more advanced computational procedures. They divided the year into 365 days, 5 hours, 49 minutes and 16 seconds, very close to the figure accepted today.</p><p>Copernicus's remark that his system explained the planetary motions with no more than 34 circles has been taken to imply that many epicycles had been added to the Ptolemaic system in an attempt to match observation, though modern computations using Ptolemy's unmodified theory have reproduced the published tables. A remark credited, probably wrongly, to Alfonso says that, had the Lord consulted him at the creation, he would have recommended something simpler.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The earliest documented reference to the tables in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> is by Geoffrey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meaux">Meaux</a> in 1320. John of Murs wrote in 1321 that he had the tables but not the canons, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-saxony">John of Saxony</a> composed new canons for the Parisian version in 1327, which were chosen for the first printed edition of 1483; a second followed in 1492. The instructions were first written in Castilian.</p><p>Georg Purbach used the tables for his Theoricae novae planetarum, and Copernicus bought a copy while at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Cracow</a>. One use of such tables was to calculate ephemerides, which astrologers used to cast horoscopes. The Prutenic Tables of Erasmus Reinhold, published in 1551, applied the Copernican model but were not widely adopted outside German speaking countries, and ephemerides based on the Alfonsine tables continued to appear until Kepler's Rudolphine Tables of 1627.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alfonsine tables” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Alfonsine Tables</b>, astronomical tables compiled at the court of Alfonso X in Toledo, remained a standard reference for European astronomers until the sixteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
